1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:01,500
Little Moments.

2
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:03,198
The Neighborhood Cleanup.

3
00:00:03,198 --> 00:00:07,542
On Saturday, Dad takes Emma, Ben, and me to a cleanup at the park.

4
00:00:07,542 --> 00:00:12,115
We join the volunteers near the entrance and get gloves, bags, and litter pickers.

5
00:00:12,115 --> 00:00:16,146
Ben immediately starts opening and closing his picker like a giant bird beak.

6
00:00:16,420 --> 00:00:19,070
Remember, pick up ordinary litter only.

7
00:00:19,070 --> 00:00:22,362
Leave sharp or unfamiliar things alone and show an adult.

8
00:00:22,636 --> 00:00:24,138
Where should we start?

9
00:00:24,364 --> 00:00:25,931
Along this path.

10
00:00:25,931 --> 00:00:28,993
Stay where I can see you, and don't reach into the bushes.

11
00:00:29,210 --> 00:00:30,275
I'll hold the bag.

12
00:00:30,275 --> 00:00:31,952
Lucy, you can use the picker.

13
00:00:31,952 --> 00:00:33,545
We'll switch in a few minutes.

14
00:00:33,820 --> 00:00:38,282
We find paper cups, snack wrappers, and a very lonely plastic spoon.

15
00:00:38,282 --> 00:00:42,439
Ben spots something shiny near the edge of the path but stops before touching it.

16
00:00:42,724 --> 00:00:44,695
I can't tell what that is.

17
00:00:44,932 --> 00:00:46,967
Let's ask an adult about that.

18
00:00:46,967 --> 00:00:50,634
Dad calls the organizer over, and we leave it for them to handle.

19
00:00:50,634 --> 00:00:53,759
Then we return to the easy things on our stretch of path.

20
00:00:53,978 --> 00:00:55,397
This looks better already.

21
00:00:55,397 --> 00:00:57,865
You can actually see the grass beside the bench.

22
00:00:58,156 --> 00:01:00,004
I found another spoon.

23
00:01:00,004 --> 00:01:01,712
Maybe they're friends.

24
00:01:01,996 --> 00:01:06,198
After our turn, we hand in the equipment and wash our hands before having a snack.

25
00:01:06,198 --> 00:01:10,146
The park isn't magically perfect, but our little section looks much tidier.

26
00:01:10,420 --> 00:01:12,182
Nice work, team.

27
00:01:12,412 --> 00:01:16,031
Ben gives his empty hands one last imaginary bird-beak snap.

28
00:01:16,031 --> 00:01:17,760
Emma and I laugh.

29
00:01:17,760 --> 00:01:21,854
It turns out a cleanup can be useful and slightly ridiculous at the same time.
